  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery details upper-level troughing near the \nHudson Bay region. To our west, ridging extends into the southern \nCanadian Prairies and is allowing for zonal flow over the northern \nplains. A 1025mb sfc high is the primary culprit for today's \nbeautiful conditions. This high pressure continues to erode the \nblanket of low stratus over the northern half of MN and WI from\nearly this morning. With plentiful sunshine expected for the \nrest of this afternoon, temperatures should reach the the upper \n50s for most sites otherwise areas from a line northwest of Mora\ndown to Benson can expect lower 50s. A band of mid-level cu \nthat exists over the Dakotas moves in this evening which belongs\nto a weak sfc low. Luckily, this low is starved of any moisture\ntherefore PoPs will remain null tonight. If you do have any \nevening plans, you may want to grab the jacket as lows dip down \ninto the upper 30s for most. A few sites north and east of a \nline from Little Falls over to Ladysmith could dabble into the \nupper 20s. Northwesterly winds will continue to decrease through\nthis evening and becoming light and variable overnight. \n\nTomorrow's forecast will be similar to what we have saw today \nalthough a few more clouds will be lingering around. The \npreviously mentioned troughing pattern slides farther east and \nwill pull its trailing surface cold front northward late Friday \ninto Saturday. This northward retreat will help some moisture \nfilter back into our neck of the woods and provide rain chances \non Saturday. Based off latest NBM guidance, the strongest \nchances for precip remain across south-central MN. As of right \nnow, any trick or treating plans do not appear to be a washout \nfrom rain but you may want to keep an umbrella handy. Saturday's\ntemps will warm into the mid to upper-50s and lows near 50. \nGiven those temps, at this point, it is safe to say we will \ndodge another 1991 Halloween Blizzard experience. \n\nThe next rain maker arrives early next week as a low develops on the \nlee side of the Rockies and is progged straight east. The current \ntrack of this low remains to our south however enough moisture looks \nto be in place to provide additional widespread rainfall. Although \nwe are still few days out, 12z ensemble guidance appears to be in \nfair agreement that most locations could see QPF of at least 0.5\" or \ngreater by Tuesday morning. As for temps in the extend period, h850 \ntemperatures continue to run at least 3-8 degrees warmer than \nseasonal normals which by this time range in low 50s/upper 40s while \nlows range in the 30s.",
